INSERT INTO "Article" ("id", "slug", "category", "country", "titleEn", "titleNe", "excerptEn", "excerptNe", "bodyEn", "bodyNe", "sources", "publishedAt", "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'slalom-singapore-hub-ai-jobs-20261103', 'global', 'Singapore',
'Slalom Opens Singapore Hub With AI Jobs in Singapore Across Cloud, Data and Engineering',
'',
'Slalom announced Singapore as its Southeast Asia hub on 3 November 2026 and confirmed hiring across Cloud, Engineering, Data and AI, and Go-To-Market teams; no headcount, deadline or salary range was announced.',
'',
$$<p>AI jobs in Singapore gained a fresh opening on 3 November 2026, when consulting firm Slalom announced Singapore as its Southeast Asia hub and confirmed it is hiring across Cloud, Engineering, Data and AI, and Go-To-Market teams in the city. The announcement, issued in Singapore on 3 November 2026, places the new hub in the Central Business District and gives it a regional brief: to connect Slalom's established work in Australia, New Zealand and Japan with growth markets across Southeast Asia.</p>
<p>Slalom said the hub is supported by the Singapore Economic Development Board (EDB). For jobseekers, the practical point is direct: the firm named four hiring areas, and it pointed applicants to its own careers site, jobs.slalom.com, rather than to any agent or third-party listing.</p>
<h2>At a glance</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Company</th><td>Slalom, business and technology consulting firm</td></tr>
<tr><th>Announcement date</th><td>3 November 2026</td></tr>
<tr><th>New hub</th><td>Singapore, Central Business District, serving as the Southeast Asia hub</td></tr>
<tr><th>Supported by</th><td>Singapore Economic Development Board (EDB)</td></tr>
<tr><th>Teams hiring in Singapore</th><td>Cloud, Engineering, Data and AI, Go-To-Market</td></tr>
<tr><th>Sectors with current demand</th><td>Financial Services, Public Sector, Retail and Consumer Goods, Energy and Natural Resources</td></tr>
<tr><th>Headcount for the hub</th><td>Not announced</td></tr>
<tr><th>Application deadline</th><td>Not announced</td></tr>
<tr><th>Salary ranges</th><td>Not announced</td></tr>
<tr><th>How to apply</th><td>Through jobs.slalom.com</td></tr>
</table>
<h2>Why Slalom chose Singapore for its Southeast Asia hub</h2>
<p>Michael Shimota, President and Regional APAC Leader at Slalom, said establishing Singapore as the Southeast Asia hub is a key element of the company's APAC expansion strategy. He said Singapore bridges the firm's mature operations in Australia, New Zealand and Japan with the growth dynamics of Southeast Asia, and that the hub will deliver local expertise backed by the firm's global network. This is a regional delivery base, not a single-country sales office, so roles are likely to touch clients and projects beyond Singapore itself.</p>
<p>Varun Kumar, Head of Sales and Go-To-Market Leader for Southeast Asia, said the firm is already seeing strong demand for work across Financial Services, Public Sector, Retail and Consumer Goods, and Energy and Natural Resources. He said Slalom builds alongside clients and partners, architecting cloud-native foundations through an AI-first lens so clients can modernise their organisations. For candidates, that points to client-facing delivery work: understanding a client's operations first, then building technology that fits them, rather than selling a fixed product.</p>
<p>Clare Lee, Vice President for Global Enterprises at the Singapore Economic Development Board, welcomed the move, saying it reflects the depth of talent companies can draw on as they grow across Southeast Asia, and that Slalom's plans will strengthen capabilities in cloud, data and AI while creating skilled job opportunities for the local workforce. The EDB backing signals official support, but it does not change the hiring facts: no post count, salary band or closing date was given.</p>
<h2>AI jobs in Singapore: what Slalom is actually hiring for</h2>
<p>The announcement names four hiring areas in Singapore: Cloud, Engineering, Data and AI, and Go-To-Market. That spread is broader than a single AI team. Cloud roles typically cover the platforms AI systems run on, engineering roles cover building software, Data and AI roles cover data pipelines and models, and Go-To-Market roles cover taking those services to clients. Applicants should read each posting on its own terms: a Data and AI post and a Go-To-Market post at the same firm can demand very different skills.</p>
<p>Slalom also said it plans to explore collaborations with local Institutes of Higher Learning to recruit and develop local talent. That suggests early-career routes may follow, but no programme, intake number or start date was announced, so nothing of that kind should be treated as open yet. The hub announcement opens a hiring direction; individual postings will define the real openings as they appear.</p>
<h2>What the announcement does not say</h2>
<p>Three gaps deserve plain stating. First, Slalom did not announce how many people the Singapore hub will employ, so no headcount figure can be quoted for it. Second, it did not announce a closing date, so there is no deadline to work towards; each posting will carry its own timing. Third, it did not announce salary ranges or eligibility rules for applicants from outside Singapore, so pay and work-permit questions cannot be answered from this announcement alone. Guessing about pay or visa eligibility is exactly how jobseekers get misled.</p>
<p>For readers outside Singapore, the work-permit question is the one to settle first. Singapore's permit rules vary by pass type, salary threshold and sector, and the announcement does not address them. The guide to <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/singapore-work-permit-eligibility-and-fees-explained-for-indian-workers-20261017">Singapore work permit eligibility and fees</a> sets out how those rules work in general, but the only binding answer for a Slalom post will be the one stated in that posting and confirmed with the employer. Do not assume a Singapore hub role is open to every nationality, and do not pay anyone who claims to secure a permit for it.</p>
<h2>What to do next</h2>
<p>First, go to the source the company itself named: jobs.slalom.com. Search for Singapore roles under Cloud, Engineering, Data and AI, and Go-To-Market, and read each posting in full before applying. Note the location, the team, the stated requirements and any closing date, because those details live in the posting, not in the hub announcement.</p>
<p>Second, match preparation to the team, not to the headline. A Cloud or Engineering applicant should be ready to show delivered systems work; a Data and AI applicant should be ready to show data and model work in a live setting; a Go-To-Market applicant should be ready to show client-facing delivery. Evidence of shipped work carries weight across AI hiring, as the round-up of <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/40-remote-ai-jobs-hiring-today-with-salaries-up-to-350k-20261009">remote AI jobs hiring with salaries up to 350,000 dollars</a> shows.</p>
<p>Third, apply only through the company's own careers site. Slalom has not announced any paid application route, and no legitimate employer charges a fee to apply. Treat any message, agent or social post that asks for payment for a Slalom interview, offer or work permit as a scam, and check the posting on jobs.slalom.com before sharing personal documents.</p>
<p>Finally, keep the hub in proportion. It is a real, dated announcement from a named firm with EDB support, and it names four hiring teams in Singapore. It is not a vacancy count, a salary offer or a visa promise. Watch the careers site for actual postings, and judge each one on its own stated terms.</p>
<p><small>Sources: Slalom announcement of 3 November 2026 as issued through <a href="https://www.globenewswire.com/news-release/2026/11/03/3393862/0/en/slalom-accelerates-apac-expansion-with-establishment-of-strategic-southeast-asia-hub-in-singapore.html">GlobeNewswire</a>.</small></p>$$, 
