NOW(), NOW(), NOW())
ON CONFLICT ("slug") DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO "Article" ("id", "slug", "category", "country", "titleEn", "titleNe", "excerptEn", "excerptNe", "bodyEn", "bodyNe", "sources", "publishedAt", "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'ai-contact-centre-jobs-south-korea-redesign-20261103', 'global', 'South Korea',
'AI Jobs in South Korea: Contact Centres Redesign Roles Instead of Cutting Staff',
'',
'Remember and Company survey findings published 3 November 2026 show only 18 per cent of South Korean firms that adopted AI in customer service cut headcount, while 29 per cent grew staffing and 45 per cent reassigned duties.',
'',
$$<p>AI jobs in South Korea are being reshaped rather than removed, according to new survey findings published on 3 November 2026. The report, from Remember and Company, found that South Korean companies which adopted AI in customer service mostly kept, reassigned or grew their contact centre staff, instead of cutting them. Only 18 per cent of organisations that deployed AI technology reported reducing headcount, while 29 per cent increased staffing levels and 45 per cent maintained their workforce and reassigned job duties.</p>
<p>The findings come from the AI Customer Experience Report, released by Remember and Company on 30 October 2026 and reported by HR Asia on 3 November 2026. The survey covered customer service professionals and executives across four sectors: manufacturing and construction, financial services, retail and e-commerce, and information technology and telecommunications. In these South Korean firms, the more common outcome was a changed job, not a lost one.</p>
<h2>At a glance</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Report</th><td>AI Customer Experience Report, Remember and Company</td></tr>
<tr><th>Report release date</th><td>30 October 2026</td></tr>
<tr><th>Findings published</th><td>3 November 2026, by HR Asia</td></tr>
<tr><th>Organisations that cut headcount after AI adoption</th><td>18 per cent</td></tr>
<tr><th>Organisations that increased staffing</th><td>29 per cent</td></tr>
<tr><th>Organisations that kept staff and reassigned duties</th><td>45 per cent</td></tr>
<tr><th>Sectors surveyed</th><td>Manufacturing and construction; financial services; retail and e-commerce; IT and telecommunications</td></tr>
<tr><th>Companies testing or using AI</th><td>74 per cent</td></tr>
<tr><th>Companies with full enterprise-wide AI deployment</th><td>6 per cent</td></tr>
<tr><th>Companies planning to increase AI investment next year</th><td>53 per cent</td></tr>
<tr><th>Vacancies announced in this report</th><td>None; this is a survey of employers, not a recruitment notice</td></tr>
</table>
<h2>AI jobs in South Korea: redesign, not replacement</h2>
<p>The gap between expectation and outcome is one of the report's sharpest findings. Among firms that had not adopted AI, 36 per cent anticipated headcount reductions before implementation. Among firms that had adopted it, only 18 per cent actually reduced headcount. Fear of replacement ran well ahead of replacement itself, at least in this survey's South Korean sample.</p>
<p>A key reason, the report suggests, is that customers keep asking for people. Half of the companies adopting AI reported frequent requests from customers to transfer to a human agent, while 45 per cent noted repeat submissions for the same query following automated responses. Automated answers often did not settle the matter, and customers pushed through to a person anyway. The automated layer handled part of the work, but the human layer still had to close the case.</p>
<p>Efficiency gains were real but uneven. While 65 per cent of respondents recorded operational gains in processing costs and response times, only 52 per cent observed improvements in customer satisfaction. Internal speed improved more reliably than the customer's experience of it. A company can truthfully say AI made its operation faster while customers still feel no better served, and the jobs that survive will be the ones that close that gap.</p>
<h2>Clear human handover rules separate the winners</h2>
<p>Operational success correlated strongly with defined boundaries between automated systems and human agents. Among firms reporting performance that exceeded expectations, 78 per cent had established clear rules for when human counsellors should intervene. Among underperforming adopters, only 32 per cent had established such workflows. Across all surveyed adopters, only 8 per cent maintained formally documented role-division guidelines, and 56 per cent cited unaddressed escalation demands as the main driver of poor outcomes.</p>
<p>Read together, those figures describe a management gap rather than a technology gap. Most firms had some sense of when a person should step in, but very few had written it down, and firms that left escalation demands unaddressed performed worst. Joo Dae-woong, head of Remember's Market Data Business Division, said adoption outcomes depend less on technical capabilities and more on establishing clear criteria for human intervention. For workers, the valuable skill is knowing when to override a tool, take over a case, and resolve what it could not.</p>
<h2>Skills are shifting towards AI tools and data</h2>
<p>The report also tracks a clear shift in what support roles demand. Operational priorities moved towards proficiency with AI tools, cited by 53 per cent, and data interpretation skills, cited by 44 per cent, while emotional response and empathy skills dropped in relative priority to 30 per cent. That does not mean empathy stopped mattering: customers asking to speak to a person are, in effect, asking for human judgement. But employers now rank tool fluency and data reading above empathy as a standalone quality, because daily work increasingly means supervising automated systems, checking their outputs, and stepping in where they fail.</p>
<p>Investment plans point the same way, and the hiring signal is echoed elsewhere: <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/ai-job-vacancies-in-the-gulf-nearly-triple-in-four-years-20261009">AI job vacancies in the Gulf nearly tripled in four years</a>. While 74 per cent of surveyed firms are currently testing or using AI, only 6 per cent have implemented full enterprise-wide deployments, so most adoption is still partial. Looking ahead, 53 per cent plan to increase AI investment over the next year, prioritising summarisation tools at 30 per cent, voice bots at 26 per cent, and real-time agent support systems at 24 per cent, all designed to assist rather than replace human counsellors.</p>
<h2>What to do next</h2>
<p>First, be clear about what this story is not. It announces no vacancies, no employer hiring drive and no application route. Anyone who wants a contact centre or AI support role in South Korea should look for actual postings from named employers, and should not treat this survey as an opening to apply for. Its value is as a skills signal.</p>
<p>Second, build the two skills the survey ranks highest: working fluently with AI tools, and interpreting the data those tools produce. Practical preparation means hands-on practice with summarisation tools, voice-bot supervision and real-time agent support systems, plus the judgement to spot when an automated answer is wrong or incomplete.</p>
<p>Third, practise the handover itself. The firms that performed best had clear rules for human intervention, so interview preparation should include examples of taking over a difficult case and resolving a repeat query. That is the work customers are still asking a person to do.</p>
<p>Fourth, keep expectations honest. The survey covers South Korean firms in four sectors only. Readers elsewhere should treat it as evidence about where service work is heading, and check local postings for pay, eligibility and language requirements. The broader <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/40-remote-ai-jobs-hiring-today-with-salaries-up-to-350k-20261009">remote AI jobs hiring now</a> round-up shows how widely AI-linked roles vary: a trend is not a vacancy.</p>
<p>Finally, beware of anyone selling this story as a job offer. No fee, agent or social post can convert a survey into employment, and no legitimate employer charges a fee to apply.</p>
<p><small>Sources: Remember and Company AI Customer Experience Report, released 30 October 2026, as reported by <a href="https://hr.asia/asia-pacific/ai-adoption-in-south-korean-contact-centres-drives-job-redesign-over-layoffs/">HR Asia</a> on 3 November 2026.</small></p>$$, 
'',
ARRAY['https://hr.asia/asia-pacific/ai-adoption-in-south-korean-contact-centres-drives-job-redesign-over-layoffs/'],
NOW(), NOW(), NOW())
ON CONFLICT ("slug") DO NOTHING;
